    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes a lot of sense regarding the LCC's flexibility. My main concern, though, is that with manuscripts this old, the cataloging process can sometimes feel a bit unpredictable. Sometimes items feel like they just \"disappear\" into the stacks or end up in these weird little gaps between categories where they don't seem to belong. Do you have any advice on how to prevent that kind of chaotic breakdown in an archive?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The unpredictable nature of cataloging manuscripts can be a challenge, especially when dealing with items that have been stored for centuries. One approach to mitigating this issue is to adopt a more \"gathering\" or \"assembling\" approach to cataloging, rather than a purely \"classifying\" one. This means that you're not just assigning a fixed classification to an item, but rather gathering together a group of related items and creating a more nuanced, contextualized understanding of their relationships.\n\nFor example, you might create a \"gathering\" of manuscripts related to a particular historical event or figure, rather than trying to fit each item into a single, rigid category. This approach can help you to better understand the complex relationships between different items, and to create a more accurate and comprehensive catalog.\n\nAnother strategy is to use a combination of both the Dewey Decimal System and the LCC, or to use a hybrid system that incorporates elements of both. This can help to create a more flexible and adaptable system that can accommodate the unique characteristics of your manuscripts.\n\nIt's also worth noting that the use of \"gathering\" or \"assembling\" approaches can be particularly effective for rare and unique items, where the traditional classification systems may not be as relevant.",
